      en: 'Social Anxiety Disorder (also termed Social Phobia) is a prevalent and disabling psychiatric condition characterized by intense, persistent fear or anxiety in social or performance situations where the individual is exposed to potential scrutiny, negative evaluation, humiliation, or rejection by others. Far beyond normal situational nervousness or introverted temperament, this condition triggers overwhelming autonomic distress during ordinary interactions, such as speaking in meetings, initiating conversations, attending gatherings, eating in public, or performing before an audience. Individuals dread that their anxiety will be visibly apparent through blushing, trembling, stuttering, sweating, or mind-blanking, interpreting these benign physiological signals as catastrophic evidence of incompetence. Social anxiety typically begins during early adolescence (median onset age 13) and, without timely clinical intervention, tends to pursue an unremitting, lifelong chronic course.',
